
  <sn><md>### Exclude in Git

`:(exclude)` is a Git `pathspec exclusion pattern`. It tells Git to exclude the `auth/` directory from the paths matched by a command.

**Syntax**

```
git <command> <pathspec> ':(exclude)<path>'
```

---

#### Examples

```
# Add all files except auth/
git add . ':(exclude)auth/'

# Show changes except auth/
git status -- . ':(exclude)auth/'

# List tracked files except those in auth/
git ls-files -- . ':(exclude)auth/'

# Add all files except auth/ and config.php
git add . ':(exclude)auth/' ':(exclude)config.php'
```

---

#### Common pathspec patterns

| Pattern                   | Meaning                                            |
| ------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |
| `:(exclude)auth/`         | Exclude the `auth/` directory                      |
| `:(exclude)config.php`    | Exclude `config.php`                               |
| `:(exclude)*.log`         | Exclude `.log` files                               |
| `:(exclude)temp/*`        | Exclude files directly inside `temp/`              |
| `:(exclude,glob)**/*.log` | Exclude `.log` files throughout the directory tree |
| `:(top,exclude)auth/`     | Exclude the root-level `auth/` directory           |

---

#### How it works

Suppose your project looks like this:

```
QDoc-Dev/
├── App/
├── auth/
├── content/
├── layout/
├── config.php
└── index.php
```

Running:

```
git add . ':(exclude)auth/'
```

stages matching files from `App/`, `content/`, `layout/`, and the root, but excludes `auth/`.

&gt; Important: This exclusion applies only to that command. It does not permanently ignore `auth/`, remove previously staged files, or stop Git from tracking files already committed.</path></pathspec></command></md></sn>
